# Searching

```python
import re

text = "Order ID: 948372"

match = re.search(r"\d+", text)

print(match.group())
```

Output

```
948372
```

---

# Why r""?

Notice

```python
r"\d+"
```

The

```
r
```

means

```
Raw String
```

Backslashes are treated literally.

Without it

```python
"\n"
```

becomes a newline.

With

```python
r"\n"
```

it remains

```
\n
```

This is why almost every regex uses raw strings.

---

# Common Regex Patterns

Digits

```python
r"\d+"
```

Word

```python
r"\w+"
```

Whitespace

```python
r"\s+"
```

Email (simple example)

```python
r"\S+@\S+"
```

---

# Finding Multiple Matches

```python
import re

text = "IDs: 10 20 30"

print(

    re.findall(

        r"\d+",

        text

    )

)
```

Output

```
['10', '20', '30']
```

---

# Replacing with Regex

```python
import re

text = "Phone: 9876543210"

print(

    re.sub(

        r"\d",

        "*",

        text

    )

)
```

Output

```
Phone: **********
```

Useful for masking sensitive data.

---

# When NOT to Use Regex

Suppose

```
user@example.com
```

Need domain.

Don't write

```python
re.search(...)
```

Instead

```python
email.partition("@")
```

Much simpler.

### Rule

Use string methods first.

Use regex only when the pattern becomes complex.

